# Android (TWA)
# The generated Gradle project is build output — `node scripts/build-twa.mjs`
# recreates it. twa-manifest.json is generated too, from the environment.
android-twa/
# Signing keys never go in a repository. Play ties an app to its key forever:
# a leaked key lets someone else ship an update to your users, and a lost one
# means republishing under a new listing.
*.keystore
*.jks


# The signing key password lives beside the key; neither belongs in git.
*.keystore.pass
*.pass.*.bak

# ── Local only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Machine-specific overrides and the files a working checkout accumulates that
# are not the product.
#
# Anything particular to ONE person's setup goes in `.git/info/exclude`
# instead, which is not part of the repository. That is the whole difference:
# this file is published, so every line in it describes something to whoever
# reads it — the editor somebody uses, the notes they keep, the container they
# work in. None of that is anybody else's business, and an ignore file is a
# strange place to announce it. `tests/ignore-files-say-nothing.test.ts` fails
# if anything named in .git/info/exclude turns up here.
#
# The leading slashes matter and are not tidiness: an unanchored pattern
# matches a directory of that name at ANY depth — back when this repo was named
# `server/`, the bare pattern also swallowed `src/lib/server/`, and every new
# file added there stopped appearing in `git status`. Two of them were missing
# from the repository before anybody noticed.
/local.mk

# Coverage reports (yarn test:coverage)
coverage/

# Dropped-in asset bundles. `static/` is served to the public verbatim, so an
# archive left there is a 1.5MB download somebody can find — and it went into
# the history once, from a `git add -A`, before anybody noticed. The one copy
# of the logo is src/lib/logo/mark.png; a delivery it came in is not an asset.
/static/*.zip
/static/*.tar.gz
